<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scandinavia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scandinavia is the region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> comprising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> Scandinavian traders used river routes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> flowed northward via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> territory, as accounts of the Khazar Khaganate note. In the early modern period Scandinavian markets imported Baltic grain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scandinavia.jpg" alt="Scandinavia" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image courtesy Jacques Descloitres, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team at NASA GSFC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scandinavia_M2002074_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
